 "title": "Teaching business models with Skittles",
 "topic": "business ideas",
 "summary": "A professor demonstrates how business models work by having students trade Skittles while the professor acts as the trade commissioner, taking a fee for every trade. The lesson highlights how platform owners and intermediaries profit from transactions regardless of who wins or loses.",
 "key_points": [
  "Students were given 20 minutes to trade Skittles to acquire as many of the same color as possible.",
  "The creator served as the 'Skittle Trade Commissioner' to ensure fairness.",
  "Every trade required reporting to the commissioner, with a fee of one Skittle per person making the trade.",
  "By the end of the 20 minutes, the creator collected a large collection of Skittles without ever trading or competing in the game.",
  "This mirrors real-world business models like credit cards taking a cut of transactions, stock exchanges collecting fees, sports team owners profiting regardless of wins/losses, and social platforms selling attention to advertisers."
 ],
 "content": "The creator explains an activity he gave his students in his Duke in Silicon Valley class. Each student received a bag of Skittles with the goal of trading to get as many Skittles of the same color as possible in 20 minutes. The creator acted as the Skittle Trade Commissioner to record trades on a spreadsheet. Every trade cost both parties a fee of one Skittle paid to the creator. By the end of 20 minutes, the creator ended up with a large collection of Skittles without trading or competing. The creator explains that this demonstrates a business model where the platform or intermediary benefits from every action, similar to Visa or Mastercard taking a cut, stock exchanges collecting fees, sports team owners profiting whether teams win or lose, and social media platforms selling user attention to advertisers while users try to get likes.",
